          %p
            %strong Noam Chomsky
            is an American
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linguistics"} linguist
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosopher
          %p He is often regarded as the greatest linguist of the 20th century and has indisputably been the most influential one.
          %p
            His seminal early work on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language"} formal language
            theory and computational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexity+theory"} complexity theory
            is by now an established part of the foundations of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://chomsky.info/"} personal page
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://linguistics.arizona.edu/user/noam-chomsky"} institute page
          %p For what it’s worth, there is of course also a:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noam_Chomsky"} Wikipedia entry
          %h2#notable_ideas Notable Ideas
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord
                  universal grammar
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/universal+grammar"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context-free+grammar"} context-free grammar
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chomsky+hierarchy"} Chomsky hierarchy
          %h2#references References
          %p Introduction of the hierarchy of formal languages via automata models of grammar:
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Three models for the description of language
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.its.caltech.edu/~matilde/Chomsky3Models.pdf"} draft
          %p
            THE classic text of modern (generative) linguistics introducing
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree representations
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Syntactic structures
          %p Introduction of features into syntax and first general constraints on grammar transformations:
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Aspects of the Theory of Syntax
          %p
            The founding text of modern generative
            %span.newWikiWord
              phonology
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/phonology"} ?
            carrying a heavy debt to structuralist linguistics:
          %ul
            %li
              N. Chomsky, M. Halle,
              %em The Sound Pattern of English
              , MIT Press 1966.
          %p Taking issue with the generative semantics “heresy” as well as laying down the foundations of contemporary phrase structure theory:
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              %em Studies on Semantics in Generative Grammar
              , Mouton The Hague 1972.
          %p
            Containing the early “debates” with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montague+semantics"} Montague semantics
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              %em Essays on Form and Interpretation
              , North-Holland Amsterdam 1977.
          %p The mature and genuine framework of generative linguistics as it evolved from 25 years of research:
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              %em Lectures on Government and Binding
              , Mouton de Gruyter Berlin 1993[1981].
          %p Many well established concepts are tossed overboard when optimality is adopted as central design feature of natural language in
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              %em The Minimalist Program
              , MIT Press 1995.
          %p The preliminarily last word is here
          %ul
            %li
              Noam Chomsky,
              %em The UCLA Lectures
              , ms. October 2020. (
              %a{:href => "https://ling.auf.net/lingbuzz/005485"}> lingbuzz
              )
          %h2#selected_publications  Selected publications
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            in generative linguistics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"}> Matilde Marcolli
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noam+Chomsky"} Noam Chomsky
                %em Mathematical Structure of Syntactic Merge
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18278"}> arXiv:2305.18278
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"}> Matilde Marcolli
                , Robert Berwick,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noam+Chomsky"} Noam Chomsky
                %em Old and New Minimalism: a Hopf algebra comparison
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.10270"}> arXiv:2306.10270
                )
